From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:16:53 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: Resume the bridges above a device before its PME is
 turned off at removal

pci_stop_dev() starts by turning the device's PME off, which reads and
writes its PM control register. Nothing has resumed the bridges above
the device at that point: the driver is only released afterwards. A
bridge in D3hot does not forward configuration requests, so when a
hierarchy whose bridges are runtime suspended is removed while its
link is up, the first device stopped, which is the one deepest and
last on its bus, gets an Unsupported Request from the bridge above it:

  pcieport 0000:01:00.0: PCIe Bus Error: severity=Uncorrectable (Non-Fatal)
  pcieport 0000:01:00.0:   [20] UnsupReq               (First)
  pcieport 0000:01:00.0: AER:   TLP Header: 0x05000001 0x00007a03 0x02200084 0x0b010000

(a two byte configuration read of 02:04.0 at 0x84, its PM control
register). Seen when the host bridge above a Thunderbolt dock, whose
bridges were idle, is removed.

Take the runtime PM reference configuration accesses from user space
take, around the access. A device that is already marked disconnected
is left alone: nothing above it answers, and its accesses fail without
reaching the bus.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/pci/remove.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/remove.c b/drivers/pci/remove.c
index e711ac1d4..7f30be05c 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/remove.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/remove.c
@@ -19,7 +19,20 @@ static void pci_free_resources(struct pci_dev *dev)
 
 static void pci_stop_dev(struct pci_dev *dev)
 {
+	bool connected = !pci_dev_is_disconnected(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * Turning PME off reads and writes the device's configuration space,
+	 * which a bridge above it in D3hot does not forward: the request then
+	 * ends as an Unsupported Request at that bridge. Devices are stopped
+	 * from the bottom of the hierarchy up, so the bridges above may well
+	 * be runtime suspended; resume them for the access.
+	 */
+	if (connected)
+		pci_config_pm_runtime_get(dev);
 	pci_pme_active(dev, false);
+	if (connected)
+		pci_config_pm_runtime_put(dev);
 
 	if (!pci_dev_test_and_clear_added(dev))
 		return;
-- 
